        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b979edef-60c8-55c7-bf30-8b3d5c6b86ee",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Gabriela Mistral, Alan Sillitoe, Geoffrey Chaucer, Thomas Wyatt, Ivo Andrić, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, Octavio Paz\n- Roles: poet-diplomats, angry young men\n- Awards: Nobel Prize in Literature\n- Topics: Latin American literature, lyric poetry\n\nFacts:\n- Alan Sillitoe was born on 4 March 1928 and died on 25 April 2010.\n- In the 1950s, Alan Sillitoe was regarded as one of the so-called \"angry young men.\"\n- Alan Sillitoe disliked the \"angry young men\" label, as did most of the other writers to whom it was applied.\n- In 1945, Gabriela Mistral became the first Latin American author to receive the Nobel Prize in Literature.\n- The Nobel Prize in Literature cited Gabriela Mistral's lyric poetry as inspired by powerful emotions and as making her name a symbol of the idealistic aspirations of the Latin American world.\n- Geoffrey Chaucer and Thomas Wyatt are among the best-known poet-diplomats.\n- The category of poet-diplomats includes Nobel Prize in Literature recipients Ivo Andrić, Gabriela Mistral, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, and Octavio Paz.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d0e7c9a9-0c67-5d6d-8d05-6505271811c3",
        "text": "Page: Gabriela Mistral. Sentence: In 1945 she became the first Latin American author to receive a Nobel Prize in Literature, \"for her lyric poetry which, inspired by powerful emotions, has made her name a symbol of the idealistic aspirations of the entire Latin American world\".\nPage: Alan Sillitoe. Sentence: Alan Sillitoe (4 March 192825 April 2010) was an English writer and one of the so-called \"angry young men\" of the 1950s.\nPage: Alan Sillitoe. Sentence: He disliked the label, as did most of the other writers to whom it was applied.\nPage: Poet-diplomat. Sentence: The best known poet-diplomats are perhaps Geoffrey Chaucer and Thomas Wyatt; the category also includes recipients of the Nobel Prize in Literature: Ivo Andrić, Gabriela Mistral, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz and Octavio Paz.",
